<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01WH">Example <span data-tag="01WH">5.4.2.2</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $q: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a morphism of simplicial sets. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $q$ is an interior fibration (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="01WG" href="/tag/01WG">5.4.2.1</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $q$ is an inner fibration (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="01BA" href="/tag/01BA">4.1.1.1</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> The implication $(1) \Rightarrow (2)$ follows from the observation that every $2$-simplex of $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is thin, and the implication $(2) \Rightarrow (1)$ follows from Corollary <a data-tag="01TC" href="/tag/01TC">5.1.1.10</a>. In particular, if either of these conditions is satisfied, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is an $\infty $-category. </p>
</article>
